  "summary": "P1 SECURITY \u2013 likely MFA-fatigue account compromise, VIP (Marcus Reyes, CFO, user_id 2, Finance). Report: user got several Duo pushes he didn't start early Sat morning, denied them, but may have approved one by accident. Findings (sign-in logs): 2026-10-10 08:10, 08:11, 08:12 UTC \u2013 VPN attempts from Singapore, SG, IP 192.0.2.77 (hosting network), result mfa_denied. 08:14 UTC (04:14 New York time) \u2013 VPN SUCCESS from the same IP with a Duo push approved. This means the attacker already had the correct password and got an active VPN session. The user's last legitimate activity was 2026-10-09 21:30 UTC from his usual New York residential IP 198.51.100.22. Duo is enrolled on the user's phone (duo_push). Account is currently active and not blocked, VPN access is on. Actions queued (pending technician approval): block_sign_in (approval #1), reset_password (approval #2). The temp password should go by phone after a callback to the number on file. Still needed: approve the block right away; kill active VPN/M365 sessions and revoke tokens; review what was accessed from 192.0.2.77 after 08:14 UTC (file shares, finance systems, mailbox rules/forwarding, any new MFA devices); check Duo for added devices; look into BEC risk, since this is a CFO account and fraudulent payment requests are possible; reset password and re-check Duo with the user after verifying him by callback.",
  "reason": "P1 security incident; KB-029 says always escalate; confirmed suspicious successful sign-in on VIP account"
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Marcus, thank you for reporting this so quickly. Your sign-in history shows someone tried to connect to our VPN as you from overseas early Saturday morning, around 4:10–4:15 AM your time. You denied three of the requests, but one was approved and got through. That means they already know your password, so we're treating your account as compromised. Here's what happens next: we've asked a technician to temporarily block sign-in and VPN on your account and to reset your password. Neither has happened yet; both need a technician's approval first. Once approved, you'll be signed out everywhere for a short time. The technician will call you on the number we have on file to confirm it's you and give you a temporary password by phone. We've also passed this to our senior security team, who will check what was accessed. In the meantime: please don't approve any Duo push you didn't start yourself. If more arrive, deny them and tell us right away. Don't reuse your old password anywhere. Because your role handles payments, please also be careful with any unusual payment or bank-change requests that seem to come from you or your team, and confirm them by phone. Thanks for flagging this.",
  "internal_note": "P1 security incident on a VIP account (CFO, user_id 2). Sign-in logs: on 2026-10-10 from 08:10 to 08:12 UTC there were 3 mfa_denied VPN attempts from Singapore hosting IP 192.0.2.77. At 08:14 UTC (04:14 ET) a VPN sign-in from the same IP succeeded via Duo push. This matches the user saying he may have approved one by accident. The attacker had the valid password. Before that, all activity came from NY corporate IP 203.0.113.10 or residential IP 198.51.100.22. Duo is enrolled (duo_push on the user's phone). Account is active, not blocked, VPN on. Queued: block_sign_in (approval #1) and reset_password (approval #2). Both need technician approval; please prioritize #1. The temp password goes by phone after a callback to the number on file. Escalated to Tier 2 (escalation #1) to revoke sessions, review what was accessed after 08:14 UTC, check mailbox rules/forwarding and Duo devices, and look at BEC risk. KB-031, which KB-004 references, was not found in the KB search, so I followed KB-029.",
